findings.body:
The regex `/\+[a-zA-Z0-9]{25}/` has no anchor at the end and no check for the CUID prefix. It matches any `+` followed by exactly 25 alphanumerics and removes only those 25 characters, which means:

- `john+abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxy@x.com` (25-char tag) becomes `john@x.com`, which is wrong.
- `john+abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz@x.com` (26-char tag) becomes `johnz@x.com`, which is corrupted. I ran this to confirm.

A CUID v1 is 25 characters and always starts with `c`, so a stricter pattern would avoid this. The same helper is used in five places, so the damage spreads to every email field.

::code{file="apps/api/v2/src/ee/bookings/2024-08-13/services/output.service.ts" lines="96-98"}

::suggestion{file="apps/api/v2/src/ee/bookings/2024-08-13/services/output.service.ts" lines="96-98"}
private getDisplayEmail(email: string): string {
  // Strip only a trailing "+c<24 alnum>" CUID right before the @ sign.
  return email.replace(/\+c[a-z0-9]{24}(?=@)/, "");
}

If the CUID format is guaranteed, the existing comparison with `google-calendar.service.ts` should be checked too, so both use the same pattern.

In `getOutputRecurringSeatedBookings`, the PR replaces `return transformed.sort(...by start time)` with `return transformed;`. This has nothing to do with `displayEmail`, and it changes the response order for recurring seated bookings. The sibling methods still sort by start, so the behavior is now inconsistent. The `bookingsIds` order from the caller is not guaranteed to be chronological, so clients that relied on the sort will see a different order.

If this was meant as a cleanup, it needs its own PR and a test showing ordering doesn't matter. Otherwise, restore the sort.

::diff{file="apps/api/v2/src/ee/bookings/2024-08-13/services/output.service.ts" lines="411-424"}

Some new assertions take the expected value from the same response they're checking. For example, `displayEmail: createdSeatedBooking.attendees[0].displayEmail` is compared to a value from a previous response of the same code. If the server returns the wrong value, the expectation moves with it, so the assertion can't fail on the bug it's meant to catch. The same pattern appears in the `attendee?.displayEmail` check in the seated-bookings spec.

The managed-user spec does this right: it compares against `firstManagedUserEmail` and `secondManagedUserEmail`, which are known inputs. Use the same approach in the seated tests.

::diff{file="apps/api/v2/src/ee/bookings/2024-08-13/controllers/e2e/seated-bookings.e2e-spec.ts" lines="277-285"}

The PR adds six entries to `.github/oasdiff-err-ignore.txt`. These tell the oasdiff CI check to ignore `oneOf` changes on booking responses. The check exists to catch unintended contract changes, and the PR does not explain why these specific changes are safe. Adding `displayEmail` as a required response property is an additive change, but the ignore entries also hide unrelated `oneOf` changes for the same endpoints. Future changes to those endpoints won't be flagged.

Narrow the ignore rules to the exact messages, or add a comment explaining why each is expected.

::diff{file=".github/oasdiff-err-ignore.txt" lines="2-8"}

The `displayEmail` / `displayGuests` block is copied almost verbatim into `getOutputBooking`, `getOutputRecurringBooking`, and the seated variants. The copies already differ slightly: one uses `.map(this.getDisplayEmail)` (unbound, which works only because the method doesn't use `this`) and another uses `(guest: string) => this.getDisplayEmail(guest)`.

Separately, `BookingAttendee.cleanEmailForDisplay` in the DTO file is never called. It duplicates the service helper, and its comment says "Validate" although it only replaces. Remove it, and pull the shared block into a single private method.

